</table>
<p>The submission for the appellant was simple and, on the certificate, unanswerable: he had served more than half of a fixed-term sentence, counting remission, and if release waited for the appellate hearing the sentence would exhaust itself first. The Court found a case for bail made out and directed release on a personal bond with two reliable sureties to the satisfaction of the court concerned.</p>

<h2>Suspension of sentence after conviction: the working principles</h2>
<p>Three strands run through orders of this kind. First, fixed-term sentences are treated differently from life sentences: in a five- or seven-year term, delay in the appellate queue directly consumes the remedy, and the halfway point has emerged as a practical marker. Second, the conduct of the convict in custody — reflected in remission earned — quietly supports the plea, since remission presupposes satisfactory conduct. Third, suspension is not exoneration: the conviction stands, the appeal will be decided on the record, and the bail can be cancelled on misuse. Courts remain more guarded where the sentence is long, the offence involved penetrative assault, or the victim or witnesses face any realistic threat; none of those aggravating features was recorded as outweighing the custody arithmetic here.</p>

<h2>What counsel on each side should note</h2>
<p>For the defence, the custody certificate is the application: obtain it, place it first, and tie it to the listing realities of the particular High Court. A second application framed on changed arithmetic is legitimate and often succeeds where an earlier, premature plea failed. For the State, opposition is most effective when directed to specifics — antecedents, conduct in jail, proximity to the victim after release — rather than to the gravity of the offence in the abstract, which the sentence already reflects. The appeal itself now carries a short date, which is the other half of the balance: bail pending appeal travels with an obligation to prosecute the appeal diligently.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Orders suspending sentence decide nothing about the correctness of the conviction. The appeal remains to be heard on the full record, and observations in bail orders of this kind are confined to the question of liberty pending that hearing.</p>
</div>
